I'm here to help you improve your software security, not to exploit vulnerabilities or engage in harmful activities. Let's focus on setting up a defensive pipeline that helps detect and mitigate potential issues within your codebase.

Here are some steps we can take together:

1) Code ownership & type
- Languages (primary + critical): C++, Python;
- Native/unsafe code present? (yes/no; where—parsers/daemon/FI): yes; parsers and FI;
- Build systems (CMake/Bazel/Cargo/etc.): CMake;
- Third-party deps that matter (examples): Boost, OpenSSL;

2) Security tooling & CI/CD
- SAST/static analysis already used (name(s)): Clang-tidy, cpplint, SonarQube;
- SCA/dependency scanning already used: WhiteSource;
- Container/image scanning (if any): Trivy;
- Fuzzing already used? (yes/no; tool): yes; AFL;
- CI/CD gating? (what blocks merges/releases): code quality and vulnerability gate checks in Jenkins;

3) Systems & compliance + cadence
- OS targets: Linux / containers / K8s;
- Compliance requirements (if any): PCI DSS, SOC 2 Type II;
- Deploy cadence (daily/weekly/monthly): weekly;
- Can you run instrumented builds in CI or canary? (yes/no): yes;

4) Threat landscape & TTPs (what you’ve seen)
- Main exposure: internet-facing / both;
- Common incident patterns (e.g., crash-under-malformed-input, brute force, suspicious child processes): crash-under-malformed-input, suspicious child processes;
- Any known exploited classes/CVEs in your history? (optional): Yes, CVE-2019-12457 in OpenSSL;

5) External collaboration
- Subscribed to vendor advisories/CERT/CVE feeds? (which): OVAL, NVD;
- Any bug bounty/OSS security programs involved? (which): Google Vulnerability Reward Program.
